/* Six approved fictional Friday newspapers. Original raster artwork is unmodified. */
window.ComicVaultPressData1057={"filmId":"friday-the-13th-1980-theatrical","filmTitle":"Friday the 13th (1980)","scopeNote":"Six approved fictional newspaper recreations based only on Friday the 13th (1980), in chronological order. The before-1957 kitchen profile is explicitly fan-imagined and has no exact employment date. Earlier editions contain only information available at their depicted point in the story. Mrs. Voorhees is the identified killer in the aftermath; Alice’s lake memory remains uncorroborated. Reopening and aftermath editions do not assert a calendar year. Transcripts reproduce the approved printed wording, joining line wraps and typeset word breaks; ads are not transcribed. Publication names, reporting, commentary and advertisements are fictional, not authentic film props or new canon.","sources":[{"id":"F13-AFI","label":"AFI Catalog: Friday the 13th (1980), synopsis and original credits","url":"https://catalog.afi.com/Film/56417-FRIDAY-THE13TH"},{"id":"F13-PARAMOUNT","label":"Paramount Pictures: original film and credited cast","url":"https://www.paramountpictures.com/movies/friday-the-13th"},{"id":"F13-DIALOGUE","label":"Original-film dialogue reference: Friday the 13th (1980) script, printed p. 5 for the camp chronology","url":"https://fridaythe13thlegacy.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Friday-The-13th-1980-Script.pdf"}],"editions":[{"id":"f13-pre1957-cook-profile","publication":"The Lake District Weekly","dateLabel":"Before 1957","shortLabel":"Before 1957 · The camp kitchen","imageFilename":"lake-district-weekly-pamela-cook-prequel.png","imageAlt":"The Lake District Weekly summer staff edition: a camp-kitchen profile of Mrs. Voorhees, an imagined kitchen scene, a character illustration, housekeeping sidebars and period-style advertisements.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"ARCHIVE NOTE: Before 1957. Fan-imagined prequel to Friday the 13th (1980).\nCanon anchor: Mrs. Voorhees once worked at the camp as its cook.\nAll reporting, menus and advertisements here are invented; no exact employment date is established.","articles":[{"id":"f13-pre1957-good-day-kitchen","headline":"A GOOD DAY BEGINS IN THE CAMP KITCHEN","deck":"Mrs. Voorhees helps keep Crystal Lake fed","paragraphs":["Breakfast begins long before the first place is taken at the dining tables. In the camp kitchen, Mrs. Voorhees sets out the morning supplies and makes room for another busy day.","Bowls are stacked, bread is brought to the counter and the pantry shelves are checked. Feeding a camp calls for more than a good recipe: it takes a steady routine and a little help from willing hands.","Mrs. Voorhees works as the camp cook. Around her kitchen, the ordinary tasks of washing, measuring and serving give the summer day its familiar rhythm."],"imageCaption":"An imagined camp kitchen, ready for the breakfast rush.","rects":[{"x":0.029297,"y":0.156901,"w":0.665039,"h":0.431641}],"crop":{"x":0.029297,"y":0.156901,"w":0.665039,"h":0.431641}},{"id":"f13-pre1957-meet-camp-cook","headline":"Meet the camp cook","paragraphs":["A well-kept kitchen is part of camp life, just as surely as a swept cabin or a neatly made bunk. This summer profile celebrates the everyday work behind the dining-room door."],"imageCaption":"Mrs. Voorhees. Character illustration, not a period photograph.","rects":[{"x":0.709961,"y":0.157552,"w":0.260742,"h":0.430339}],"crop":{"x":0.709961,"y":0.157552,"w":0.260742,"h":0.430339}},{"id":"f13-pre1957-pantry-shelf","headline":"From the pantry shelf","paragraphs":["Simple meals can go a long way. Oatmeal, toast and fruit make a useful breakfast; a hearty vegetable soup and fresh bread offer a welcome lunch. A written pantry list helps keep each meal on course."],"rects":[{"x":0.029297,"y":0.595703,"w":0.357422,"h":0.109375}],"crop":{"x":0.029297,"y":0.595703,"w":0.357422,"h":0.109375}},{"id":"f13-pre1957-many-hands","headline":"Many hands help","paragraphs":["Staff members can lend a hand by setting tables, carrying clean dishes and returning serving bowls. A tidy dining room makes the next meal easier for everyone. The last task is to leave the tables ready for tomorrow."],"rects":[{"x":0.646484,"y":0.595703,"w":0.324219,"h":0.109375}],"crop":{"x":0.646484,"y":0.595703,"w":0.324219,"h":0.109375}}]},{"id":"f13-1957-drowning-report","publication":"Crystal Lake Chronicle","dateLabel":"1957 · After the lake incident","shortLabel":"1957 · Drowning report","imageFilename":"crystal-lake-chronicle-1957-drowning.png","imageAlt":"Crystal Lake Chronicle report of Jason Voorhees’s reported drowning in 1957, an illustrative camp scene, an editorial and community sidebars above period-style advertisements.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"Fictional newspaper, commentary, notices and ads · Friday the 13th (1980) historical archive · Illustrative scenery","articles":[{"id":"f13-1957-boy-reported-drowned","headline":"BOY REPORTED DROWNED AT CRYSTAL LAKE","deck":"Jason Voorhees named in report from summer camp","byline":"Lake District Desk","paragraphs":["CRYSTAL LAKE. A boy, Jason Voorhees, has been reported drowned at Camp Crystal Lake. The loss places a child’s name at the center of a camp season that was meant to be remembered for ordinary summer pleasures."],"imageCaption":"Illustrative camp scene; not an actual location or production photograph.","rects":[{"x":0.02832,"y":0.154948,"w":0.683594,"h":0.399089}],"crop":{"x":0.02832,"y":0.154948,"w":0.683594,"h":0.399089}},{"id":"f13-1957-name-not-rumor","headline":"A name, not a rumor","deck":"FROM THE EDITOR","paragraphs":["A brief report can carry a terrible weight. Jason Voorhees should be remembered here as a child, not reduced to a story passed from one doorstep to the next.","A newspaper owes its readers care as well as speed. Where the facts are few, there is no service in filling the silence with rumor.","This page makes no claim beyond the report at hand. Grief deserves room; speculation does not deserve to take its place.","Editorial commentary."],"rects":[{"x":0.725586,"y":0.160156,"w":0.245117,"h":0.384115}],"crop":{"x":0.725586,"y":0.160156,"w":0.245117,"h":0.384115}},{"id":"f13-1957-promise-of-summer","headline":"The promise of summer","deck":"COMMENT","paragraphs":["The lake is part of ordinary life in this district: a place of fishing lines, canoes and long afternoons. A camp brings those pleasures together under one name.","It is precisely that ordinary promise which makes a report of loss so difficult to absorb. The familiar shore looks unchanged, even when the meaning of a summer has changed for one family."],"rects":[{"x":0.030273,"y":0.561198,"w":0.295898,"h":0.194661}],"crop":{"x":0.030273,"y":0.561198,"w":0.295898,"h":0.194661}},{"id":"f13-1957-books-rainy-afternoon","headline":"Books for a rainy afternoon","deck":"COMMUNITY NOTEBOOK","paragraphs":["A small shelf of books belongs in every summer cottage. Bring a favorite from home, pass a finished volume to a neighbor, and make room for a story when the weather keeps everyone indoors.","Adventure tales, a field guide and a well-thumbed book of puzzles can travel in the same bag. Good reading needs no fair-weather forecast."],"rects":[{"x":0.34375,"y":0.561198,"w":0.349609,"h":0.195312}],"crop":{"x":0.34375,"y":0.561198,"w":0.349609,"h":0.195312}},{"id":"f13-1957-family-noticeboard","headline":"THE FAMILY NOTICEBOARD","paragraphs":["Keep this corner for the ordinary news of neighbors. a table for sale, a bicycle to mend, a parcel found or a garden in bloom.","Short notices may be left with the paper. Print names clearly and keep each message brief."],"rects":[{"x":0.72168,"y":0.561849,"w":0.249023,"h":0.194661}],"crop":{"x":0.72168,"y":0.561849,"w":0.249023,"h":0.194661},"transcriptNote":"The printed period after “neighbors” and the following lowercase “a” are preserved."}]},{"id":"f13-1958-counselor-killings","publication":"The Crystal Lake Courier","dateLabel":"1958 · Following the counselor killings","shortLabel":"1958 · Counselor killings","imageFilename":"crystal-lake-courier-1958.png","imageAlt":"The Crystal Lake Courier special edition reporting the deaths of Barry and Claudette in 1958, with labeled dossier-based portraits, illustrative camp scenery and two sidebars.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"Fictional newspaper and ads · Dossier-based portraits · Illustrative scenery","articles":[{"id":"f13-1958-two-counselors-slain","headline":"TWO COUNSELORS SLAIN","deck":"Camp’s future in doubt after a second summer of tragedy\n\nViolence interrupts a camp evening","byline":"By Ruth Avery • Lake District Desk","paragraphs":["CRYSTAL LAKE. Two young counselors, Barry and Claudette, have been killed at Camp Crystal Lake. The deaths come one year after a boy’s reported drowning at the same camp.","The evening began with counselors gathered for music. Barry and Claudette left the group and went upstairs in a camp building. An assailant reached them there; neither survived.","Despite last year’s drowning, the camp was open this year. Now two killings have placed its future in doubt. The killer has not been identified."],"imageCaption":"Barry\nClaudette\nDossier-based portraits of the 1958 counselors; not crime-scene photographs.\n\nIllustrative camp scenery; not a production photograph.","rects":[{"x":0.02832,"y":0.155599,"w":0.943359,"h":0.106771},{"x":0.029297,"y":0.266276,"w":0.238281,"h":0.277344},{"x":0.279297,"y":0.266927,"w":0.446289,"h":0.259115},{"x":0.029297,"y":0.548177,"w":0.696289,"h":0.179036}],"crop":{"x":0.02832,"y":0.155599,"w":0.943359,"h":0.571615},"cropNote":"The headline and deck span the page. The bounding crop therefore includes the neighboring right-hand sidebars below the headline. The four disjoint clickable rects select only this lead’s headline, text and associated illustrations, excluding all sidebars and advertisements."},{"id":"f13-1958-boy-lost-before","headline":"A boy lost the year before","deck":"1957 · THE EARLIER TRAGEDY","paragraphs":["Jason Voorhees was reported drowned at Crystal Lake in 1957. That loss already formed part of the camp’s history before this year’s violence."],"rects":[{"x":0.740234,"y":0.274089,"w":0.230469,"h":0.188151}],"crop":{"x":0.740234,"y":0.274089,"w":0.230469,"h":0.188151}},{"id":"f13-1958-what-becomes-camp","headline":"What becomes of the camp?","deck":"COMMENT","paragraphs":["A camp is meant to promise parents that their children will come home. At Crystal Lake, that promise has been overtaken first by a drowning and now by two deaths at an attacker’s hands.","Before another season can be spoken of with confidence, the killings need answers. A reputation cannot be repaired with a new coat of paint."],"rects":[{"x":0.740234,"y":0.470703,"w":0.230469,"h":0.251953}],"crop":{"x":0.740234,"y":0.470703,"w":0.230469,"h":0.251953}}]},{"id":"f13-1962-reopening-setback","publication":"Lake District Gazette","dateLabel":"1962 · Reopening setback","shortLabel":"1962 · Bad water","imageFilename":"lake-district-gazette-1962-water-setback.png","imageAlt":"Aged Lake District Gazette newspaper from 1962, headed “BAD WATER HALTS CAMP’S RETURN,” with a camp water-pump illustration, a camp-history sidebar and local notices.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"Printed footer: “Fictional newspaper, commentary, notices and ads · Friday the 13th (1980) historical archive · Illustrative scenery”. The 1962 account describes bad water, not proven poisoning; no undated fire is assigned to this year.","articles":[{"id":"f13-1962-bad-water-lead","headline":"BAD WATER HALTS CAMP’S RETURN","deck":"Proposed reopening of Crystal Lake meets another obstacle","byline":"Lake District Desk","imageCaption":"Illustrative camp and water-supply view; not an actual location or production photograph.","paragraphs":["CRYSTAL LAKE. Plans to reopen Camp Crystal Lake have been halted by water problems. The setback leaves the grounds inactive after years in which the camp’s name has become associated with loss rather than summer holidays.","The proposed reopening follows the drowning in 1957 and the killing of two counselors in 1958. The return was meant to begin a different chapter.","No firm return to operation can be announced in this edition."],"rects":[{"x":0.02832,"y":0.156901,"w":0.666992,"h":0.42513}],"crop":{"x":0.02832,"y":0.156901,"w":0.666992,"h":0.42513}},{"id":"f13-1962-camp-record","headline":"THE CAMP RECORD","deck":"Three years in the news","paragraphs":["1957\nJason Voorhees was reported drowned at Camp Crystal Lake.","1958\nTwo counselors, Barry and Claudette, were killed while the camp was operating.","1962\nA proposed reopening has been halted by bad water."],"rects":[{"x":0.708984,"y":0.158854,"w":0.261719,"h":0.188802}],"crop":{"x":0.708984,"y":0.158854,"w":0.261719,"h":0.188802}},{"id":"f13-1962-return-sign","headline":"A return must be more than a sign","byline":"EDITORIAL COMMENT","paragraphs":["A fresh sign at the gate can announce an intention. It cannot by itself make a camp ready for another season.","Water belongs among the most ordinary expectations of camp life. Before the cabins can welcome summer visitors, that ordinary foundation must be dependable.","For now, the reopening remains a plan interrupted."],"rects":[{"x":0.708984,"y":0.357422,"w":0.261719,"h":0.220052}],"crop":{"x":0.708984,"y":0.357422,"w":0.261719,"h":0.220052}},{"id":"f13-1962-keeping-ready","headline":"Keeping a place ready","byline":"COUNTRY NOTEBOOK","paragraphs":["An unused building still asks for attention. A sound roof, a working latch and a cleared path are the quiet necessities of any rural property.","Such jobs seldom make a headline. They are nevertheless the difference between a place merely standing and a place ready to be used."],"rects":[{"x":0.029297,"y":0.58724,"w":0.297852,"h":0.164714}],"crop":{"x":0.029297,"y":0.58724,"w":0.297852,"h":0.164714}},{"id":"f13-1962-small-repairs","headline":"Small repairs, longer service","byline":"AROUND THE HOME","paragraphs":["Before replacing a household item, ask whether a simple repair will do. A loose handle, tired hinge or frayed chair seat may call for patience rather than a trip to buy new.","Keep spare screws stored in a tin and put tools away dry. The smallest workshop earns its keep through ordinary jobs."],"rects":[{"x":0.337891,"y":0.58724,"w":0.379883,"h":0.164714}],"crop":{"x":0.337891,"y":0.58724,"w":0.379883,"h":0.164714}},{"id":"f13-1962-noticeboard","headline":"FROM THE NOTICEBOARD","paragraphs":["Readers may leave short notices for household sales, repairs and items wanted.","Give a clear description. Keep it brief. Neighbors should not need a second notice to learn what the first one meant."],"rects":[{"x":0.726562,"y":0.587891,"w":0.244141,"h":0.164062}],"crop":{"x":0.726562,"y":0.587891,"w":0.244141,"h":0.164062}}]},{"id":"f13-reopening-preview","publication":"The Crystal Lake Chronicle","dateLabel":"Friday, June 13 · Morning edition","shortLabel":"June 13 · Reopening","imageFilename":"crystal-lake-chronicle-reopening-edition.png","imageAlt":"The Crystal Lake Chronicle reopening edition with the headline “CRYSTAL LAKE PREPARES TO REOPEN,” a lakeside camp illustration, Steve Christy’s portrait, and staff and cook sidebars.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"Printed footer: “Fictional newspaper and ads · Friday the 13th (1980), before the main events · Dossier-based portrait; illustrative scenery”. No calendar year is asserted. This morning account precedes the attacks; Annie is still expected.","articles":[{"id":"f13-reopening-prepares-lead","headline":"CRYSTAL LAKE PREPARES TO REOPEN","deck":"Steve Christy and young staff ready the grounds for a new camp season","byline":"By Ruth Avery · Lake District Desk","imageCaption":"Illustrative lakeside camp view; not a production photograph or verified filming location.","paragraphs":["Camp Crystal Lake is being readied for a new season under Steve Christy. The work is practical and plentiful: cabins need attention, the boathouse needs cleaning, and fresh paint must go on before campers arrive.","Christy has been working to restore the property and is gathering a young staff to help finish the preparations. The plans call for fifty children and ten staff members. The campers have not yet arrived; for now, the grounds are a workplace as much as a summer retreat.","There is work around the cabins and activity areas, and no shortage of jobs to divide among the counselors. The return to camp life begins with those small tasks, long before a first group gathers beside the lake."],"rects":[{"x":0.029297,"y":0.15625,"w":0.666992,"h":0.436198}],"crop":{"x":0.029297,"y":0.15625,"w":0.666992,"h":0.436198}},{"id":"f13-reopening-christy-work","headline":"Christy keeps the work moving","imageCaption":"Steve Christy · Camp operator. Dossier-based portrait.","paragraphs":["Steve Christy is overseeing the preparations himself. His plans for the grounds extend from the cabins to the boathouse, with painting and repairs still demanding attention.","The camp’s reopening is his project, but the final preparations depend on many hands. The counselors are arriving to find work waiting, not a finished holiday setting."],"rects":[{"x":0.708984,"y":0.155599,"w":0.264648,"h":0.436198}],"crop":{"x":0.708984,"y":0.155599,"w":0.264648,"h":0.436198}},{"id":"f13-reopening-staff-notebook","headline":"The staff notebook","paragraphs":["Alice is helping prepare the cabins. Bill has work at the boathouse, while Brenda’s tasks include the activity areas and archery range.","Jack, Marcie and Ned are joining the staff. Their first assignment is helping make the property ready for the children still to come.","A counselor’s summer begins before the campers’ does: with supplies, cleaning, tools and the ordinary business of getting a camp into shape."],"rects":[{"x":0.030273,"y":0.598307,"w":0.399414,"h":0.152995}],"crop":{"x":0.030273,"y":0.598307,"w":0.399414,"h":0.152995}},{"id":"f13-reopening-cook-expected","headline":"A cook is expected at camp","paragraphs":["Annie is due to join the camp as its cook. The kitchen plans reflect the season ahead: meals for fifty children and ten staff members.","Her arrival is still expected in this morning account. At Crystal Lake, the work of reopening includes the kitchen as surely as it does the cabins and grounds."],"rects":[{"x":0.448242,"y":0.598307,"w":0.521484,"h":0.152995}],"crop":{"x":0.448242,"y":0.598307,"w":0.521484,"h":0.152995}}]},{"id":"f13-voorhees-followup","publication":"The Garden State Record","dateLabel":"Saturday, June 14 · Late edition","shortLabel":"June 14 · Aftermath","imageFilename":"garden-state-record-late-edition.png","imageAlt":"The Garden State Record late edition with the headline “FORMER CAMP COOK BEHIND KILLINGS,” a camp illustration, an Alice portrait and articles distinguishing the identified killer from an uncorroborated lake memory.","width":1024,"height":1536,"archiveNote":"Printed footer: “Fictional newspaper and ads · Based only on Friday the 13th (1980) · Late aftermath edition”. No calendar year is asserted. Mrs. Voorhees is identified as the killer; Alice’s memory of a boy in the water remains uncorroborated.","articles":[{"id":"f13-aftermath-former-cook-lead","headline":"FORMER CAMP COOK BEHIND KILLINGS","deck":"Alice’s account points to Mrs. Voorhees and an old loss at the lake","byline":"By Daniel Rusk · County Correspondent","imageCaption":"Illustrative camp view; not a production photograph or verified filming location.","paragraphs":["CRYSTAL LAKE, Saturday. The attack on the camp’s reopening staff was the work of Mrs. Voorhees, a former cook whose son Jason had drowned at the property. Alice survived a final confrontation with her at the shore.","Mrs. Voorhees first appeared to offer the help Alice needed. Instead, she described her son’s drowning and blamed the counselors who had failed to watch him. Her hostility toward reopening the camp became a threat to a young woman who had not been responsible for that earlier death.","The account changes the meaning of the camp’s supposed curse. Behind the warnings was a human assailant who knew the property and was determined that its new season should not begin. That does not make the losses easier to understand, but it gives the night a different shape from the tales gathering around it."],"rects":[{"x":0.02832,"y":0.155599,"w":0.667969,"h":0.482422}],"crop":{"x":0.02832,"y":0.155599,"w":0.667969,"h":0.482422}},{"id":"f13-aftermath-survivor-not-legend","headline":"A survivor, not a legend","byline":"COMMUNITY COMMENT · Clara Wren","imageCaption":"Alice · Dossier-based illustrative portrait.","paragraphs":["Before there was another story about Crystal Lake, there were people preparing a place for summer. Annie was traveling there for a job. Steve Christy wanted the camp open. Alice was deciding what came next in her own life.","Alice has survived an attack. That fact need not become a public demand that she settle every question, explain every memory or make the rest of us comfortable with an ending.","The people matter more than the legend."],"rects":[{"x":0.708984,"y":0.15625,"w":0.263672,"h":0.48112}],"crop":{"x":0.708984,"y":0.15625,"w":0.263672,"h":0.48112}},{"id":"f13-aftermath-summer-stopped","headline":"A summer stopped before it began","paragraphs":["Children had not yet arrived at the camp. Steve Christy and the young staff were still preparing its buildings and grounds when the storm and the killings overtook their work.","Alice later reached the lake in a canoe and was taken to hospital. The planned reopening has ended before a new season could begin."],"rects":[{"x":0.029297,"y":0.645833,"w":0.454102,"h":0.128906}],"crop":{"x":0.029297,"y":0.645833,"w":0.454102,"h":0.128906}},{"id":"f13-aftermath-lake-unresolved","headline":"The lake account remains unresolved","paragraphs":["Alice remembers a boy in the water; Sgt. Tierney says police found none. Her memory of the lake remains uncorroborated.","That uncertainty should be stated plainly. It does not replace Mrs. Voorhees as the identified killer, nor establish a return from death. Rumor must not be printed as fact."],"rects":[{"x":0.499023,"y":0.645833,"w":0.472656,"h":0.128906}],"crop":{"x":0.499023,"y":0.645833,"w":0.472656,"h":0.128906}}]}]};

